1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,534
Try to tickle yourself right now. Nothing happens.

2
00:00:03,534 --> 00:00:06,981
But when someone else does it, you can't stop laughing.

3
00:00:06,981 --> 00:00:12,685
This isn't a party trick. It's one of the deepest clues we have about how your brain works.

4
00:00:12,685 --> 00:00:17,888
And it starts with a machine your brain runs every second of your life: prediction.

5
00:00:18,488 --> 00:00:21,786
Your brain is not a camera. It's a prediction engine.

6
00:00:21,786 --> 00:00:25,957
Every movement you make, it predicts the feeling before it arrives.

7
00:00:25,957 --> 00:00:29,816
When the feeling matches the prediction, the brain cancels it.

8
00:00:29,816 --> 00:00:34,858
That's why you can't tickle yourself: the surprise is deleted before you feel it.

9
00:00:34,858 --> 00:00:42,016
Scientists proved it with a robot: add a tiny delay between your movement and the tickle, and suddenly, it tickles.

10
00:00:42,016 --> 00:00:45,875
Your own touch, half a second late, feels like someone else's.

11
00:00:46,475 --> 00:00:48,590
So why does tickling exist at all?

12
00:00:48,590 --> 00:00:54,748
One theory: it's a training game. Play-fighting and tickling teach children which touches are safe.

13
00:00:54,748 --> 00:00:59,040
The laughter is a signal: this is a threat simulation, keep learning.

14
00:00:59,040 --> 00:01:04,514
It's also why tickling only works where you're vulnerable: the ribs, the neck, the feet.

15
00:01:04,514 --> 00:01:07,438
Your body is rehearsing defense without danger.

16
00:01:08,038 --> 00:01:12,300
Here's the strange part. The same cancellation runs your whole life.

17
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:15,686
You don't feel your socks until someone mentions them.

18
00:01:15,686 --> 00:01:18,318
You don't hear your fridge until it stops.

19
00:01:18,318 --> 00:01:23,271
The brain erases everything it predicted, to save attention for what it didn't.

20
00:01:23,271 --> 00:01:28,850
Which means you never experience the world as it is. Only the errors in your predictions.

21
00:01:29,450 --> 00:01:34,359
So the next time someone tickles you and you laugh, remember what it really is.

22
00:01:34,359 --> 00:01:36,472
Not a reflex. A failed prediction.

23
00:01:36,472 --> 00:01:39,454
Your brain, caught off guard, for half a second.

24
00:01:39,454 --> 00:01:44,985
And that tiny gap between what you expect and what you feel is where being alive happens.

25
00:01:44,985 --> 00:01:48,838
So ask yourself: what else are you predicting away, right now?
